e apotonic  and after 12 hours of treatment within  blasting about 21 percent of the cells  are apototic so we can use this plot to  create our apototic index and measure  that over time to really quantify the  effects of them blasting  on these  subcellular components  as measured by their their morphology  acute lymphoblastic leukemia is  characterized by an overabundance of  immature b cells  in this experiment we'll be looking at t  cells modified with a chimeric antigen  receptor that can target these cd19  positive b cells and induce cytotoxic  effects  so here crispr cast 9 was used to  enhance cultured lymphocytes to target  the cd19b cells  car t cells were also incubated with and  without icam which can be used as a  blocking agent and for the image stream  experiment we'll fix the cells we'll  label the t cells with either cd8 or cd4  and the b cells with cd19 and compare  which of the t cells either the cd8s the  cd4s or the cd84 combination does the  best job of identifying the target b  cell  we can then quantify conjugate formation  and the cytotoxic effects as well as  look at icann blocking  to analyze this experiment we want to  begin  by characterizing the t cell subsets in  this sample  so thymocytes will express either cd4  single positive t cells cd8 positive t  cells or early thymocytes  can express cd4 and cd8 simultaneously  after gating on these three different  car t subsets we can compare their  ability to bind to b cells  and induce cytotoxic effects so in the  middle series of dot plots you can see  approximately 18 percent of the cd4 cd8s  are binding to  the b cell looking at the corresponding  imagery in the top panel you can see  these cells are double positive for cd4  and 8 and they're attached to the b cell  about fourteen percent of the cd4  positive car t cells are binding to the  b cell  uh and  about seven percent of the cd8 positive  car t cells are binding to the b cell  so after compiling the data from that  analysis we can now start to compare the  effects of treatment with icam  and look at that over each subset of car  t cell  so if you look at the cd4 cd8 car t cell  that seems to show the greatest affinity  for the target b cell  if you look at the cd8 only positive car  t cell seems to have a relatively low  affinity for the b cell and clearly icam  inhibits car t binding in each of the t  cell subsets  so utilizing the image cytometry and the  imagery we can not only characterize car  t binding its target cell  we can also look at cytotoxic effects  and downtrend downstream experiments and  we can use the imagery to really  characterize the effectiveness of our  car t cells when treatment with  cancer such as al  so that was the last example that i had  showing the benefits of imaging flow  cytometry when analyzing your  immuno-oncology experiments next i'll  turn it over to katie gillis who has  some great applications that she  describes using the guava muse systems  thank you  the guava muse and guava easy site  systems have many applications for the  immuno oncology market we are now going  to discuss some of those applications  the guava news and easy site instruments  use a patented micro capillary system  that provides a simple and reliable  design  the micro capillary eliminates the need  for shea fluid in our faux cytometers  utilizing our patented fiberglass faux  cell  the laser light hits the flow cell  it allows for scanning of every particle  that flows through the system  we then combine this with a positive  displacement syringe pump  allowing us to determine absolute counts  because we know exactly how much fluid  flows through the system  every acquisition  in addition to the accurate cellular  counting the  micro capillary and syringe pump design  provides  the technology allows for small sample  sizes which preserve your precious  cellular samples and produce low  biohazardous waste  the muse and easy site technologies  provide pre-validated kits that allow