d talk about  extracellular vesicles or evs  evs are membrane-derived structures that  include exosomes microvesicles and  apoptotic bodies  exosomes range from about 30 nanometers  to 100 nanometers  exosomes have been shown to transfer  molecules between cells and have the  potential to transfer signals between  cells  exosomes are released under normal  physiological conditions however  exosomes are also believed to serve as  mediators in the pathogenesis of  neurological vascular hematological and  autoimmune diseases as well as cancer  quantifying and characterizing evs is  challenging due to their small size  high magnification microscopy such as em  can be used to analyze ebs however it's  very low throughput  additionally pmt-based flow cytometers  have a difficult time detecting evs  since they are not highly sensitive  imaging flow cytometers that use a  charge coupled device or ccd camera can  detect ebs such as the cell strain  in order to immunophenotype evs we  follow this workflow using red blood  cells or rbcs and platelet-derived evs  the evs are obtained from solarcus  biosciences the rbcs and platelets were  washed and treated with a calcium  ionophore to induce fasciculation  the vesicles were then isolated via  centrifugation  the resulting evs were labeled with  either an  anti-cd235 antibody with pe  or a cd41 antibody with apc  the labeled ev samples were serial  diluted as shown here  samples were then acquired on the cell  stream for three minutes per sample with  100  laser power  all proper controls were run including  antibody only buffer only and detergent  controls  these controls were run exactly as the  eb samples  samples were run in duplicate on three  cell stream instruments  shown here in the top row is the gating  strategy for identifying the potential  ev populations for both the rbc and  platelet-derived evs  the population ranges from about 50 to  200 nanometers with a peak  around 100 or 120 nanometers with the  platelet evs